<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palembang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palembang is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musi-river">Musi River</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. It was the core capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a>, controlling shipping through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> straits. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedukan-bukit-inscription">Kedukan Bukit inscription</a> of 682 CE, found nearby, is the earliest known record of the Srivijayan state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palembang.jpg" alt="Palembang" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gaudi Renanda · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LRT_Palembang_Ampera_Bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Palembang occupies a wide lowland east of the Bukit Barisan Mountains in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, at an average elevation of about 8 metres and some 105 kilometres from the coast at the Bangka Strait. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musi-river">Musi River</a>, one of the largest in Sumatra, runs through it and divides the city into Seberang Ilir, the downstream bank, and Seberang Ulu, the upstream bank. The city stands where two major tributaries, the Ogan and the Komering, join the Musi, and in the rainy season the tide of the river floods many areas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest inscription found in Palembang, the Kedukan Bukit Inscription, is dated 682 CE. It tells of a king who acquires magical powers and leads a large force over water and land, setting out from the Tamvan delta and arriving at a place called Matajap, and, in the reading of some scholars, founding the polity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>. Matajap is believed to be Mukha Upang, a district of Palembang. George Coedes wrote that in the second half of the 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> were united under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendra</a> ruler reigning in Java, with his centre at Palembang.</p><p>Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a> fled the city after Javanese forces crushed it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> then plagued it. In 1407 the returning treasure fleet of Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> confronted the pirate Chen Zuyi there. A local Chinese informant revealed Chen's plan for a surprise attack, and in the battle that followed the Ming fleet destroyed the pirate fleet and killed 5,000 of its men. Chen was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> and executed, and Shi Jinqing was installed as Palembang's ruler.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> became the dominant religion after a Demak nobleman, Geding Suro, fled to Palembang with his followers and founded a new dynasty. The Sultanate of Palembang was proclaimed in 1659, and its Grand Mosque was built between 1738 and 1748. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> abolished the sultanate in 1825 and made the city the capital of the Residency of Palembang. It was chartered as a city on 1 April 1906.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Palembang is the capital of the Indonesian province of South Sumatra and the second most populous city of Sumatra after Medan. It is known for songket, a hand-woven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth patterned with gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> thread, which was used at court under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>. Local cooking relies on freshwater fish and prawn from the Musi, and pempek is among its best-known dishes. The city co-hosted the 2018 Asian Games with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palembang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and the Pala dynasty of Bengal, reached the Ganges, completed the conquest of Rajarata in Sri Lanka, and in 1025 launched naval raids on Srivijaya and the Burmese kingdom of Pegu; an inscription says he captured or plundered 14 places, identified with <b>Palembang</b>, Tambralinga and Kedah among others.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Strait of Malacca had been dominated by Srivijaya, centred on <b>Palembang</b>, until the Cholas weakened it in the 11th century, and by the end of the 13th by the Javanese Singhasari and Majapahit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Anjukladang inscription of 937 rewards a village for repelling invaders from Malayu, that is Srivijaya, and in 990 Dharmawangsa launched a naval invasion to capture <b>Palembang</b>: Java's envoys of 992 told the Chinese court of continuous war, and Srivijaya had recovered by 1003.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ming restrictions on private overseas trade (the haijin ban) gave tribute missions a privileged position, and the court's own ships sailed to Ayutthaya in Siam, Malacca, <b>Palembang</b>, Java, Patani and Annam; letters and documents of these voyages survive in the Rekidai Hoan, the kingdom's diplomatic archive.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kedukan Bukit inscription of 682 CE, found near modern <b>Palembang</b> and written in Old Malay, among the earliest surviving examples of that language, records the ruler Dapunta Hyang Sri Jayanasa leading a military expedition and establishing his authority over the surrounding region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its earliest known stone inscription, found by the Dutchman C. J. Batenburg in 1920 at Kedukan Bukit on the Tatang River near <b>Palembang</b> in South Sumatra, is written in the Pallava variety of the Grantha alphabet and dated 1 May 683.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
